WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.069 --> 00:00:14.090
Hi. In a speaking exam, you may be asked to
share an opinion or have a discussion with

00:00:14.090 --> 00:00:19.390
a partner about a topic. In this video, we’ll
have a look at how to express your opinions

00:00:19.390 --> 00:00:22.880
and how you can agree or disagree with someone
else’s point of view.

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:25.119
Let’s have a look

00:00:25.119 --> 00:00:28.800
at some phrases you can use to express your
opinions.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:33.900
Some good phrases to use for expressing your
opinion are ‘I think’, ‘I believe’,

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:39.180
‘I like’, and ‘in my opinion’. For
example, if you were talking to your friend

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:44.789
about movies, you might say ‘I think that
action movies are the best’. You can also

00:00:44.789 --> 00:00:49.539
express yourself with the negative forms of
these phrases. ‘I don’t like horror movies’,

00:00:49.539 --> 00:00:54.539
or ‘I don’t believe the world needs any
more Transformers films’ are good examples

00:00:54.539 --> 00:00:59.170
of sentences to express opinion.

00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:05.530
Do you want to make your statements stronger?
You can use adverbs and adjectives to do this.

00:01:05.530 --> 00:01:10.950
Instead of ‘I believe’ or ‘in my opinion’
you can use ‘I strongly believe’ ’ or

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:16.530
‘in my honest opinion’. Adverbs and adjectives
are useful for showing you’re serious about

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:17.400
a topic.

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:24.659
As well as expressing your opinion, it is
a good conversation skill to ask about other

00:01:24.659 --> 00:01:29.680
people’s opinions. Phrases like ‘What
do you think about…’ and ‘How do you

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:35.079
feel about’ are good questions to keep a
conversation going. You might ask someone

00:01:35.079 --> 00:01:40.439
‘what’s your opinion on climate change?’,
or use a question after you make a statement

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:45.140
- ‘I think the blue jacket is the nicest.
What do you think?’

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:52.630
Knowing how to agree and disagree with someone
else’s opinion is just as valuable as being

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:57.850
able to express your own opinion. You can
use short statements to show you feel the

00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:03.990
same as the speaker. For example, if a friend
says ‘I think football is more fun than

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:10.610
tennis’, you could respond with ‘I agree!’
or ‘that’s right!’, or ‘me too’.

00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:15.060
You can disagree with a statement in a similar
way. You could say ‘That’s not right’,

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:20.550
‘I don’t think that’s very good’,
or simply ‘I disagree’. If you have mixed

00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:25.639
feelings on an issue you can use a more neutral
phrase. For example, ‘I’m not sure about

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:29.460
that’, or ‘I partly agree with you’.

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:38.140
It’s normal to explain your opinions as
part of a discussion. If your friend says

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:42.550
‘I think the LearnEnglish Teens channel
is great’, don’t just respond with your

00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:47.859
opinion alone - use a statement to justify
it. You could say ‘me too; they have amazing

00:02:47.859 --> 00:02:52.960
presenters!’, or ‘I agree. It’s way
more interesting than reading textbooks!’

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:13.280
For more tips on learning English and preparing
for your exam, subscribe to our channel and

00:03:13.299 --> 00:03:17.449
visit the LearnEnglish Teens website where
you’ll find loads more learning material

00:03:17.449 --> 00:03:18.999
to help you improve your English!

